The '''Maroon resettlement''' ({{Langx|an|reasentamiento de marrons}}), alternatively known as the '''Maroon removal''' ({{Langx|an|remoción de marrons}}) was a [[W:Forced displacement|forced displacement]] policy in place in [[Bayara]] from 1809 to 1838. It became official policy of the Bayaran secessionist [[Xunta Nacionalista]] following its denunciation of the [[Articles of Confederation]] in 1828. The policy is considered a consequence of actions first by the [[Reapers' War|Illypnian colonists]] and then later on by the [[W:Bandeirantes|Bayaran settlers]] seeking to exploit the unexplored interior of [[Abyala]] during the [[Colonial Balisca|colonial era]] and then through the [[Maroon Wars]]. Removal policies gained popularity as tensions festered within Bayaran society regarding the [[W:Abolition of slavery|abolition of slavery]] by the [[Southern Confederation]] via the 1809 [[Granyana Convention]].

After the official adoption of the policy by the Xunta, approximately 150,000 [[W:Freedmen|freedmen]] and [[W:Free persons of color|free persons of color]], and 60,000 members of the [[Honú-Abá]], [[Murí people|Murí]] (''costeños''), [[Aruanãyará]], [[Roáka'á]], and [[Kalútupí]] [[Indigenous nations in Balisca|nations]] (which earlier resisted displacement from their ancestral homelands), were expelled from Bayara to the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. Growing support in Bayara for the forced resettlement intensified the [[Confederal Question|confederal crises]] of the post-independence years, ultimately leading to the [[Basilio mutiny]] and the subsequent [[Baliscan Civil War]]. The resettlement policy was revoked in 1838 with the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]], and the new [[Anyos Federales|Reconstruction]] government facilitated the [[W:Right of return|right to return]] for the displaced, along with reparations (in the form of [[W:Land redistribution|land redistribution]]), the recognition of indigenous sovereignty, and constitutional guarantees of cultural and political rights for all communities, with aggressive confederal protection.

In 21st century [[W:Postcolonialism|postcolonialist]] studies it has been widely citied and universally condemned as an early example of state-sanctioned {{W|ethnic cleansing}} or [[W:Settler genocide|settler genocide]] through violent {{W|population transfer}}s.

